Found during k90 (worker), unverified. A run killed inside `git reset --hard` can leave the worktree mixing new files with HEAD (and index) still at the old commit. The next online resolve repairs it (reset --hard restores exactly – verified in k84), but an offline fallback serves the dir as is, labelled with the old version (rev-parse HEAD). Fix idea: before serving a fallback, verify the worktree matches HEAD (`git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no` empty, plus untracked check if cheap) and otherwise `reset --hard HEAD` locally (no network) under the sync lock. Also micro-edge from k90: a run killed inside `remote add` between its two config writes leaves remote.origin.url without a fetch refspec; repair() sees the matching URL and leaves it, only the abbreviated-SHA fallback `git fetch origin` is affected – check `remote.origin.fetch` in repair() too if cheap. Test: plant a mixed worktree, remote unreachable → fallback serves HEAD content exactly.

